.TH PS 1
.SH NAME
ps \- report processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ps
.RB [ \-AeaNrlf ]
.RB [ \-p " pid,..."]
.RB [ \-u " uid,..."]
.RB [ \-t " tty,..."]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without selectors, ps shows processes with the caller's real user ID and
controlling terminal. Each kernel record contains IDs, state, CPU counters,
and mapped data/heap/stack page counts; kernel addresses are not exposed.
.PP
States: R running or runnable, S sleeping, D uninterruptible I/O, T stopped,
F being forked, Z zombie. TIME is the process's own user plus kernel CPU time,
not time accumulated by its children. TTY is the numeric controlling terminal:
1 is the console; 2 through 5 are PTY slaves ttyp0 through ttyp3. Zero means none.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-A, \-e
Select all processes.
.TP
.B \-a
Select processes with a controlling terminal.
.TP
.B \-r
Restrict selected processes to running/runnable states.
.TP
.B \-N
Invert selection.
.TP
.BI \-p " pid,..."
Select process IDs.
.TP
.BI \-u " uid,..."
Select real user IDs or user names. -U is an alias.
.TP
.BI \-t " tty,..."
Select numeric terminal minors, tty names, or device paths.
.TP
.B \-f
Include UID, parent PID, process group and session ID.
.TP
.B \-l
Also include private/shared KiB, scheduling priority, and nice value.
.PP
MAP-K equals PRIV-K plus SHRD-K. Shared pages appear in every referencing
process; these values must not be summed as physical memory consumption.
Code images and reserved kernel RAM are not included. Use free for unique
physical totals and top for sampled CPU usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
free(1), top(1), uptime(1)
